#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125427} :activity/id #uuid "521e104e-5661-5821-9c14-dd4e83bf463e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049929 :topic/id #uuid "1f448400-122e-5249-bf7b-b62de80111ee" :topic/math-academy-id 1971} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125427 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125793 :tutorial/content "Let's consider the following matrix:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis is a $3\\times 3$ matrix, yet it has only *two* distinct eigenvalues, namely $\\lambda_1 = 0$ and $\\lambda_2 = 1.$ Note that $\\lambda_1$ is a simple root of the  characteristic polynomial and $\\lambda_2$ is a double root.\n\nTo diagonalize a $3\\times 3$ matrix, we need to find a basis of $\\mathbb R^3$ consisting of linearly independent eigenvectors of $A.$ So the question is, can we diagonalize this matrix even though it does not have three distinct eigenvalues?\n\nLet's start by computing the eigenvectors of $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- First, we consider the simple root $\\lambda_1 = 0.$ Solving the equation $A \\mathbf x = \\lambda_1 \\mathbf x$ in the usual way, we obtain a single linearly independent eigenvector:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Next, we consider the double root $\\lambda_2 = 1.$ Let's solve the equation $A \\mathbf x = \\lambda_2 \\mathbf x$ by reducing $(A - \\lambda_2 I)$ to row-echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{2}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere is one pivot column in the reduced matrix above (the $3$rd one). Thus, $x_1$ and $x_2$ are free variables. From the first equation, we obtain the solution $x_3 = 0.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere $x_1, x_2\\in \\mathbb R.$ Thus, we have obtained *two* linearly independent eigenvectors:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSo $A$ has three linearly independent eigenvectors that form a basis of $\\mathbb R^3.$ Therefore, $A$ is diagonalizable! \n\nDiagonalizing [math]A[/math] in the usual way, we get the following:\n\\[\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\end{bmatrix}}_{A} = \n\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0 \\end{bmatrix}}_{P}\n\\cdot\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix} 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\end{bmatrix}}_D\n\\cdot\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0 \\end{bmatrix}^{-1}}_{P^{-1}}\n\\]\n\nIn terms of eigenspaces, $A$ is diagonalizable because the sum of the dimensions of the eigenspaces equals the number of rows/columns of $A.$ The eigenspaces corresponding to $\\lambda_1 = 0$ and $\\lambda_2 = 1$ are given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \n\nIf we sum the dimensions of $V_0$ and $V_1,$ we get precisely $3\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dim(V_{0}) + \\dim(V_{1}) = 1 + 2 = 3 \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}\n$$\n\nwhich means that $A$ is diagonalizable.\n\nIt's important to realize that not all matrices are diagonalizable! Let's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "10637b3f-57b1-5542-b802-95ed60bc127e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3579 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "7203c46b-0f37-5a6e-85bc-dc8132e299ee" :step/math-academy-id 20129 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125593}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125428 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124535 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124344 :question/id #uuid "2ae53015-78d7-521e-8e53-edf656a0a906" :question/math-academy-id "e-5507"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "706fbf71-9085-506e-8f45-d1edf5965be1" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048195}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125059} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125060} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272047} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272048} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323900} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323903} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323928} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360739} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360740}] :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a 3x3 Matrix Given Part of the Diagonalization"} :step/id #uuid "d22be7b1-e8f1-595d-84b2-66ed4214d0aa" :step/math-academy-id 20130 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125537}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125454 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124551 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124360 :question/id #uuid "9610299d-5fe4-5fc2-abbc-c7dcadd5164e" :question/math-academy-id "e-5893"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "99c24109-b1e9-56e8-977a-a86d5896e70a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050376} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050857}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125093} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125094} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272051} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360734} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360735} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360736} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382627} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404919}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Diagonal Matrix Corresponding to a Given Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "49e8e51d-acc1-5a76-a66a-fb1090aa4de1" :step/math-academy-id 21531 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125594}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125537 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124598 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124410 :question/id #uuid "6cc93bd0-c52c-56cf-bac4-ffcd67151dce" :question/math-academy-id "e-8495"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8ca065c-acc8-5ef2-9662-02114a90e620" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125054} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125095} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272053} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360741} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360742} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360743} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360744} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382634} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404926}] :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a 3x3 Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "3bf129cc-3d82-5925-8bb2-c86441063274" :step/math-academy-id 31599} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125593 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125856 :tutorial/content "Let's now consider the following matrix:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nIts eigenvalues are $\\lambda_1=1$ (simple root) and $\\lambda_2=2$ (double root).\n\nLet's see how many linearly independent eigenvectors we can find for this matrix.\n\n- First, we consider the simple root $\\lambda_1 = 1.$ Solving the equation $A \\mathbf x = \\lambda_1 \\mathbf x$ in the usual way, we obtain a single linearly independent eigenvector:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Next, we consider the double root $\\lambda_2 = 2.$ Let's solve the equation $A \\mathbf x = \\lambda_2 \\mathbf x$ by reducing $(A - \\lambda_2 I)$ to row-echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{2}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $3$rd). Thus, $x_2$ is a free variable. From the first and second equations, we have\n\n$$\nx_1 = x_3 = 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere $x_2\\in\\mathbb R.$ Thus, we have obtained *only one* linearly independent eigenvector:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSo $A$ has *only two* linearly independent eigenvectors, and these cannot form a basis of $\\mathbb R^3.$ Therefore, $A$ is *not* diagonalizable.\n\nIn terms of eigenspaces, the matrix $A$ is not diagonalizable because the sum of the dimensions of the eigenspaces is *smaller* than the number of rows/columns of $A.$ The eigenspaces corresponding to $\\lambda_1 = 1$ and $\\lambda_2 = 2$ are given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \n\nIf we sum the dimensions of $V_1$ and $V_2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\dim(V_{1}) + \\dim(V_{2}) = 1 + 1 = 2 \\neq 3 \\quad {\\color{red}{\\boldsymbol\\times}}\n$$\n\nwhich means that $A$ is *not* diagonalizable." :tutorial/id #uuid "c685cb77-5f99-589a-abac-5ff2154edb7d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6190 :tutorial/title "An Example of a Non-Diagonalizable 3x3 Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "d2466c9e-46cb-5a71-ace7-8154518e92f3" :step/math-academy-id 37526 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125602}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125594 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125857 :tutorial/content "A square matrix $A$ is diagonalizable if there exists a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA = PDP^{-1}.\n$$\n\nSo, diagonalizing $A$ means finding matrices $D$ and $P$ that satisfy this equation. \n\nFor example, let's diagonalize the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo diagonalize a $3 \\times 3$ matrix, we follow a three-step **diagonalization algorithm.**\n\n**Step 1:** Find the eigenvalues of the matrix.\n\nWe solve the characteristic equation $\\vert A - \\lambda I \\vert = 0.$ For the given matrix, the eigenvalues are \n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{\\lambda_1}} = {\\color{red}{\\lambda_2}} = {\\color{red}2}\\qquad \\textrm{and} \\qquad{\\color{blue}\\lambda_3 =1}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Find a basis (if it exists) consisting of eigenvectors of the matrix.\n\nFollowing the usual method for computing eigenvectors, we get \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\ncorresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\color{red}{\\lambda_1}} = {\\color{red}{\\lambda_2}} = {\\color{red}2}$ and ${\\color{blue}\\lambda_3 =1},$ respectively.\n\n**Watch out!** In this example, the total number of linearly independent eigenvectors equals the number of rows/columns of the matrix. If we obtain fewer eigenvectors at this step, then the matrix is *not* diagonalizable.\n\n**Step 3: ** Construct the matrices $D$ and $P.$\n\nNow that we have our eigenvalues and corresponding eigenvectors, we can construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and invertible matrix $P.$\n\nThe matrix $D$ has the eigenvalues of the matrix as its entries on the main diagonal. We can write them in any order, so we let\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThen, the columns of $P$ are the corresponding eigenvectors. We must write each eigenvector in the *same* column as its respective eigenvalue in the matrix $D.$ So, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A=PD{P}^{-1} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** When diagonalizing a diagonalizable matrix, we can place the eigenvalues into the matrix $P$ in any order. We just need to be sure that the diagonal entries in the matrix $D$ correspond to the columns of $P.$\n\nFor example:\n\n- Swapping the columns $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2,$ we get another valid diagonalization: \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Swapping the columns $\\mathbf{v}_2$ and $\\mathbf{v}_3$ above gives another valid diagonalization: \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "5d7a1ece-ba11-5a1b-9775-8396ebb35e65" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6191 :tutorial/title "The Diagonalization Algorithm"} :step/id #uuid "bd809b96-88bc-52f1-aa25-a46de9be7037" :step/math-academy-id 37527 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125428}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125602 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125862 :tutorial/content "To check whether diagonalization is possible, we can use the following theorem:\n\n> *An $n \\times n$ matrix is diagonalizable if and only if the sum of the dimensions of the eigenspaces equals $n$.*\n\nThat is, if \n\n$$\n\\lambda_1, \\: \\lambda_2, \\: \\ldots, \\: \\lambda_k\n$$\n\nare the distinct eigenvalues of an $n \\times n$ matrix, and the corresponding eigenspaces are \n\n$$\nV_{\\lambda_1}, \\: V_{\\lambda_2}, \\: \\ldots, \\: V_{\\lambda_k}\n$$\n\nthen the matrix is diagonalizable if and only if\n\n$$\n\\dim(V_{\\lambda_1}) + \\dim(V_{\\lambda_2}) + \\cdots + \\dim(V_{\\lambda_k}) = n.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "9b1d1c74-27ca-5336-8e15-c998929c5bd0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6259 :tutorial/title "Determining Whether an NxN Matrix Is Diagonalizable"} :step/id #uuid "9bbc4b38-3c9a-5994-849a-2fdf65233679" :step/math-academy-id 37957 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125454}}] :activity/title "Diagonalizing a 3x3 Matrix in the General Case" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124756}]}
